        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 813,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 788,
            "height": 856,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Turns a tensor of numbers into shares of a whole that favour the SMALLEST values: every value comes out positive, they add up to 1, and the lowest score gets the biggest share.\n\nIt is softmax of the negated scores - e to minus each value, divided by the sum of e to minus all of them. Use it when the scores are costs or distances rather than merits: the nearest target, the cheapest option, the joint with the least error should get the most weight.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.softmin <dim: int>\nDefault dim 0.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe scores. Receiving them triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor first. An integer tensor is refused (the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\"): convert it with t.to float32.\n\ndim (an inlet, with a box on the node):\nWHICH AXIS the shares are taken across. Default 0, or the first argument. The values along that axis sum to 1; every other axis is a separate set. For a (frames, joints) tensor, dim 1 gives each frame's distribution across the joints; dim 0 gives each joint's distribution over time. Those are entirely different questions, and getting dim wrong is the usual softmax mistake. For a 1-D tensor, as in the demo, 0 (or -1) is the only axis. Changing dim does not trigger the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe shares, the same shape as the input, summing to 1 along dim.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe same eight scores as on the softmax page: 1, 2, 0.5, 3, 1.5, 0, 2.5, 1. Here the 0 (sixth) wins with 0.35 at scale 1, the 0.5 is next with 0.21, and the 3 gets only 0.02 - the softmax picture reversed. The float shows the sum: always 1.\n\nTHE SCALE SLIDER IS THE TEMPERATURE:\nOnly the DIFFERENCES between the scores matter - adding the same amount to every score changes nothing. Multiplying them all by a number does: a large multiplier stretches the differences, so the SMALLEST score takes nearly everything and the result is almost a hard choice; a multiplier near 0 shrinks them, so every element gets nearly the same share (1/8 = 0.125 here). Dividing by a \"temperature\" before a softmax is the same adjustment; the slider multiplies by 1 over the temperature.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.softmax favours the largest. t.min finds the smallest value itself. t.cdist gives the distances softmin is often fed. t.nn.log_softmax is the logarithm of softmax.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
